      "strings": {
        "defendIntro": "Go through the signs in the colleague's inbox and decide what to do with each one. Some mean someone else is in the account, and some are ordinary, and it is not always obvious which from the first line. Take them at your own pace.",
        "allPhishRight": "Every sign was caught, with every reason named.",
        "teamLine": "By reporting or checking {n} of these rather than carrying on, you got a taken-over account looked at sooner.",
        "weakestTells": "The signs most often missed: {names} ({counts} each).",
        "weakestTell": "The sign most often missed: {names} ({counts}).",
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Quayholt Insurance, a fictional company. First you're on the red team: look at what's public about a Quayholt colleague, then write one email, dressed up as the IT service desk, that asks them to sign in again. Then you swap seats. The colleague clicked, and now you're the one whose account is being used. Sort the signs that follow. Only that second part is scored.",
        "flipHeading": "Now it lands on you",
        "flipIntro": "This is your email as Ayaan met it. He is the claims handler you sent it to, and this time he signed in.",
        "flipDefenderLine": "The service desk never sends sign-in requests by email, whatever address they come from; that is the one thing that would have given it away.",
        "flipNoSend": "Your email didn't go out this time, so there's nothing of yours to show. Next, you're the one whose account is being used, sorting the signs that follow.",
        "flipMissed": "Ayaan didn't act on it. A detail didn't match what he'd been told, or the pressure to hurry made him stop and check, and that is how many attacks fail. The one this episode is about did land, in a better-made email. Next, you're the one whose account is being used, sorting the signs that follow.",
        "flipMissedHeading": "Nothing to replay this time",
        "flipLandedNoTells": "It landed clean, with nothing in it to catch this time. Next, you're the one whose account is being used, sorting the signs that follow.",
        "allRight": "You read every sign the right way. They all trace back to one email built to look like the service desk, and the real desk never asks you to sign in by email.",
        "noBlameLine": "Everyone clicks something eventually, and a colleague who reports their own slip is doing exactly the right thing. A miss here just shows where to look next time; nobody is in trouble for reporting.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: if you think you have clicked, signed in or spotted something odd on your account or a colleague's, report it straight away, change your password on the real site, and leave the device as it is. Reporting fast, even your own slip, is what limits the damage, and report it using the route in the box below.",
        "defendOnly": "These signs were set off by an email built to look right, and anyone can be caught by one. Anything worth another look is shown below, with what gave each one away.",
        "conceptPanel": [
          {
            "heading": "The five questions a report answers",
            "text": "A useful report answers five questions, as far as you know: what you noticed, when, where (which account, device or system), who is affected, and what you have done so far. Be honest about the last one: 'I clicked the link', 'I signed in', 'I restarted it'. A rough report now beats a perfect one later; the team will ask for the rest."
          },
          {
            "heading": "First steps and recovery",
            "text": "If you clicked or signed in: report it, change the password on the real site (not through any link), then change it anywhere you used the same one, and follow the service desk. For anything on a device: leave it as it is. Do not switch it off unless you are told to, do not delete the evidence, and do not try to fix it. If the service desk asks, unplug the network cable or turn off Wi-Fi and leave it on."
          }
        ]
      },
